<description>
Expand the sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing with thick bars between adjacent matching dots.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- Foreground pixels lie on logical rows and columns separated by background spacer rows/columns.
- A logical row or column is present when it contains at least one foreground pixel.

Rule
- Expand every background spacer row/column to thickness 2.
- Expand the logical rows/columns equally so the full output is 26x26.
- Paint each foreground logical pixel as a solid rectangle of its color.
- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels horizontally through the intervening spacer.
- Connect adjacent same-color logical pixels vertically only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run.

Key insight
- The input is a sampled line drawing: horizontal same-color runs define the bars first, and only unclaimed same-color vertical neighbors are joined.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "foreground pixels on a background-spaced logical lattice",
  "dst": "expanded 26x26 lattice with 2-pixel background spacers",
  "transform": "expand logical rows/columns to equal bands; paint each foreground pixel as a solid rectangle; join adjacent same-color pixels horizontally, and join vertical neighbors only when neither endpoint is part of a horizontal same-color run",
  "grid_shape": "fixed 26x26"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  description: >
    Expand a sparse dot lattice into a 26x26 drawing. Foreground dots on a
    logical grid become thick colored rectangles; adjacent same-color dots are
    joined by bars (horizontal first, vertical only for unclaimed endpoints).
  input_grid:
    shape: (2R+1) x (2C+1) where R, C each from {1,2,3,4,6,8}
    observed_range: R in 2-4, C in 1-4 [observed]
    background: 0
    structure: |
      Even rows/cols are all-bg spacers; odd rows/cols hold the lattice dots.
      Every odd row has >=1 fg pixel; every odd col has >=1 fg pixel.
  lattice:
    dimensions: R x C (the logical dot grid)
    fill_rate: 88-100% foreground [observed]
    distinct_fg_colors: 2-6
    bg_cells_allowed: yes, but every row and column must have >=1 fg cell
    horizontal_connections: 0-4 same-color adjacent pairs per example [observed]
    vertical_connections: 0-1 unclaimed vertical pairs per example [observed]
  output_grid:
    shape: always 26x26
    band_size: (24 - 2*N) / N per axis, guaranteed integer by choice of R, C
    spacer_thickness: 2 (bg between bands)
  connection_rule: |
    H-first: horizontally adjacent same-color dots merge through the spacer.
    V-second: vertically adjacent same-color dots merge ONLY if neither
    endpoint participates in any horizontal same-color adjacency.

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct (input is small; output is deterministic from input)
  steps:
    - Pick R from {2,3,4} and C from {1,2,3,4} (band_size >= 4).
    - Create (2R+1) x (2C+1) grid filled with bg=0.
    - Fill odd-row/odd-col lattice cells with random non-zero colors (2-6 distinct).
      Optionally leave a few cells as bg (0-2 cells), ensuring every odd row and
      every odd col retains >=1 fg cell.
    - Include at least one horizontal same-color adjacency for task interest.
    - Optionally include a vertical same-color pair where neither endpoint has a
      horizontal same-color neighbor, to exercise the V-connection rule.
    - Verify _axis_spans divisibility (guaranteed by R,C choice).
```
